<td>Although Euclid never displays numbers except as lines, the Pythagoreans before him evidently did, that is, they displayed numbers as figures. The figures were in various shapes, such as triangles, squares, and so forth. Definitions 16 through 19 deal with figurate numbers, but without the figures.  Euclid defines a plane number as a number which is the product of two numbers.  Remember that for Euclid, 1 is the unit, not a number, so prime numbers are not plane numbers, but every composite number can be a plane number in at least one way.</td>

